   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   CHANGELOG (2026-08-23, v1.1 tuning pass -- on-device tested and confirmed)
     - DROP SHOT tightened to 10ms/10ms (was 17/15), 120fps-locked tempo.
     - RAPID FIRE dial widened 1-60 -> 1-100 and its press-time floor
       dropped 17ms -> 5ms, so the dial now tracks real rate 1:1 up to
       100rps instead of plateauing at ~29rps.
     - BLOOM REDUCER added (concept from a competitor build, reimplemented
       from scratch in the feroz engine's own accumulator style) -- not yet
       wired to the OLED menu, tune via BR_* defines below.
     - AUTO TRACK magnitude brought to parity with AIM ASSIST's peak via
       TRACK_MICRO (was ~3x weaker at the same orbital speed).
     - v1.3: AUTO TRACK's real fix -- ferozF37's commit was silently
       SKIPPING set_val() on any tick where the whole-unit delta rounded to
       zero (routine near sin/cos zero-crossings), so the RS output was an
       intermittent stutter-step, not a continuous signal, no matter how
       big TRACK_MICRO made the magnitude. Now commits unconditionally
       every tick. This is the actual mechanism fix; v1.1's TRACK_MICRO
       and v1.2's (reverted) dither were both treating the wrong problem.
     - RS KEEPALIVE DITHER tried and REVERTED (2026-08-23): a tiny always-on
       dither was added to test whether it would keep AUTO TRACK's assist
       armed without a real stick touch (on-device A/B testing had shown
       the "needs touch" symptom on both aim-engine architectures, ruling
       out a script bug). On-device it did NOT fix that -- AUTO TRACK still
       needed a real touch -- and only added a visible shake, so it was
       pulled in v1.2. Do not re-add this exact approach; the root cause
       needs a different fix, not a synthetic dither layered on the orbit
       that already wasn't enough on its own.
   ############################################################################ */

define OUT_PC   = 0;
define OUT_PS5  = 1;
define OUT_XBOX = 2;
define OUTPUT        = OUT_PC;   
define START_PROFILE = 1;        
define USER_LOOK = 39;
define USER_ADS  = 8;
define USER_LOOK_V = 39;
define USER_ADS_V  = 8;
define CFG_STAMP   = 0;
define CFG_VERSION = 8;
/* INSTANT RESET step time - set on request 2026-08-23 (was 10ms, already a
   120fps-locked tuning matching the ARC/Neptune competitor consensus of
   11ms; research found nobody in the field runs a 60fps-safe value here).
   8ms pushes further past even the 8.3ms/120fps frame floor - if a customer
   on 60fps (or even a strict 120fps lock) reports resets not registering,
   10-11ms is the researched-safe fallback, not a guess.                   */
define RESET_MS   = 8;
define HAIR_GATE  = 2;

/* -- Feroz Aim Engine v2 tuning ------------------------------------------
   AA_HOLD    : |right stick| below this = full assist.
   AA_RELEASE : |right stick| above this = AA_FLOOR (assist never fully dies). Between the two the
                assist fades out linearly, so a flick never "pops" off --
                that hard on/off edge is what the old build felt like as jitter.
   AA_ATTACK / AA_DECAY : gain envelope steps per ms (0..100 scale).
   AA_YSQUASH : vertical radius as a % of horizontal -- an ellipse tracks
                the way targets actually move and halves the visible bob.
   AA_MICRO   : orbit radius as a % of AA STRENGTH. A tight, fast micro shake
                trips the game's rotational assist by stick VELOCITY, so a
                smaller circle spun faster pulls harder while wandering the
                crosshair far less than the old wide, slow orbit.            */
define AA_HOLD     = 10;
define AA_RELEASE  = 95;
define AA_FLOOR    = 45;
define AA_ATTACK   = 25;
define AA_DECAY    = 4;
define AA_YSQUASH  = 60;
define AA_MICRO    = 60;

/* -- BLOOM REDUCER (test build, added 2026-08-23) --------------------------
   Concept ported from a competitor build (Serum Goat) that ran a
   magnitude-gated circular micro-orbit on the RS while ADS+FIRE are both
   held: full strength near centre, fading to zero near full deflection,
   plus a hard recentre inside a small dead zone. That source packs its
   logic as opaque hex/switch indirection and is not safe to port
   verbatim, so this is a clean reimplementation of the same shape in the
   feroz engine's own accumulator style -- not a copy of anyone's code.
   Not wired into the OLED menu yet -- tune the four values below and
   re-flash to test; BR_ENABLED=0 fully disables it.                      */
define BR_ENABLED   = 0;   /* v1.4: disabled by default -- on-device feedback flagged the
                               overall aim profile as "bloated" with unwanted micro shakes
                               once this was stacking with AA + boosted AUTO TRACK during
                               ADS+Fire. Set back to 1 to re-enable/tune, define stays. */
define BR_STRENGTH  = 20;
define BR_SPEED     = 900;
define BR_DEADZONE  = 10;

/* -- AUTO TRACK -----------------------------------------------------------
   Rebuilt 2026-08-23 (see the full rationale at the AUTO TRACK block below)
   as a pure independent orbit, not a memory of the player's own stick -
   an on-device test found the previous "slewed memory" design dragged the
   crosshair onward for ~220ms after releasing the stick, by construction
   (it fed the player's live stick reading in as its own target). Modeled
   on how BraveAim/BraveAimers.com's RS aim-assist runs (research/script
   library/Famous Sellers/FN/Unreal Script.gpc): an orbit added on top,
   never derived from the player's input, so it cannot drag on release.
   TRACK_PCT  : sustain gain, % applied as the orbit's magnitude.
   TRACK_HIT  : extra gain, %, while a rumble hit-echo confirms a real
                target under the crosshair.
   TRACK_YPCT : vertical tracking as a % of horizontal - targets move
                sideways far more than they move up and down.
   TRACK_ORBIT_SPD : angular speed of the orbit, centi-degrees/ms.
                2026-08-23: 900 -> 1320 (matching AIM ASSIST's own max
                speed at AA SPEED=100 - see "120 + feroz2*12" below). There
                is no stick-touch gate anywhere on this orbit - it posts
                unconditionally every tick regardless of input - but a slow
                orbit is a WEAK one (trip velocity = speed x radius), and a
                weak synthetic stick signal can come out too gentle for
                Fortnite's own rotational assist to register as real
                turning on its own. A real player's soft touch adding on
                top was enough to cross that threshold, which is why it
                looked "touch-gated" even though the code never gates on
                touch. Matching Apex's own proven ceiling here rather than
                inventing a new number.                                    */
define TRACK_PCT   = 80;
define TRACK_HIT   = 60;
define TRACK_YPCT  = 35;
define TRACK_ORBIT_SPD = 1320;
/* TRACK_MICRO -- magnitude-parity fix (2026-08-23, on-device finding):
   AUTO TRACK matched AIM ASSIST's angular speed (TRACK_ORBIT_SPD above)
   but never matched its raw posted magnitude. AA's peak per-tick post is
   ~STRENGTH(5) * sin(100) * envelope(100)/100 * AA_MICRO(60)/100 = ~300,
   and AA reliably engages Fortnite's own rotational assist with the stick
   sitting dead centre because ADS/Fire alone ramps its envelope to 100.
   AUTO TRACK's peak was only TRACK_PCT(80) * GAIN(115)/100 = ~92 -- about
   a third of AA's punch through the same accumulator/IIR commit -- which
   is almost certainly why it needed a real stick nudge stacked on top to
   cross the same threshold AA crosses on its own. TRACK_MICRO closes that
   gap: 92 * 326/100 ~= 300, matching AA's peak magnitude, same speed. */
/* v1.4: TRACK_MICRO removed. Real find (2026-08-23): dug up Apex's own
   ORIGINAL pre-rewrite AUTO TRACK (outputs/scripts/_archive_pre_engine_v2_
   2026-08-09/Apex_v1.0.gpc:530-536) -- the version the customer remembers
   as "always just worked, no touch needed." It was never a pure synthetic
   generator: it read the player's own RAW stick (get_ival), gated on
   `abs(raw) > 2`, and AMPLIFIED whatever tiny real signal was there. The
   exact same pattern -- raw-stick read, small deadzone gate, amplify --
   turned up independently in Serum Goat's own RS tracker (research/script
   library, function V_026: `if (abs(get_ival(RX)) > DeadZone) amplify`).
   Two independent real, shipped Fortnite scripts agree: the "always on"
   feel isn't from defeating the game's live-input check, it's from
   amplifying a real hand's natural resting micro-tremor -- which is
   virtually never PERFECTLY zero while actively gripping a controller --
   above a threshold low enough that it almost always clears. A fully
   synthetic, zero-correlation-to-the-player signal (this session's orbit,
   even boosted 3x via TRACK_MICRO, even continuity-fixed in v1.3) isn't
   the same thing that pattern is keying off. See TRACK_AMP_* below --
   that's now the primary mechanism; the orbit stays as a small ambient
   floor, not the main driver, which is also why TRACK_MICRO's 3x boost
   (a real contributor to the "bloated" shake complaint) is gone. */
define TRACK_AMP_DEADZONE = 2;
define TRACK_AMP_GAIN     = 220;

/* -- HEAD LOCK ------------------------------------------------------------
   HL_GATE  : rumble level that counts as a confirmed hit.
   HL_HOLD  : ms the confirmation stays alive after the last hit echo.
   The per-mode boost / lean rate / lean cap live in ferozF41..ferozF43.   */
define HL_GATE     = 2;
define HL_HOLD     = 450;

